IN THE COURT OF THE LEARNED ADDITIONAL DISTRICT JUDGE NO. __, JAIPUR CITY

Civil Suit No. ______ of 2025

Ramesh Kumar Sharma                                                      … Plaintiff / Applicant

Versus

Suresh Chand Agarwal & Another                                           … Defendants / Non-Applicants

APPLICATION UNDER ORDER 39 RULES 1 AND 2 READ WITH SECTION 151 OF THE CODE OF CIVIL PROCEDURE, 1908
FOR TEMPORARY INJUNCTION / STATUS QUO

MOST RESPECTFULLY SHOWETH:

1. That the applicant has filed the accompanying suit for specific performance of Agreement to Sell dated 15.01.2024 and permanent injunction.

2. That a strong prima facie case exists in favour of the applicant. The Agreement is admitted, part consideration has been paid, and the applicant has proved continuous readiness and willingness.

3. That the balance of convenience lies in favour of the applicant. If the defendants are not restrained, they may further alienate the suit property, rendering the decree infructuous.

4. That the applicant will suffer irreparable injury which cannot be compensated in terms of money if the temporary injunction is not granted.

5. That Defendant No. 1 has already committed breach by executing a subsequent sale deed in favour of Defendant No. 2. There is every likelihood of further transfer.

PRAYER

It is therefore most respectfully prayed that this Hon’ble Court may be pleased to:

(a) Restrain the defendants, their agents and any person claiming through them from alienating, transferring, selling, mortgaging or creating any third-party interest in the suit property described in Schedule-A of the plaint, till the final disposal of the suit;

(b) Direct the parties to maintain status quo with regard to title and possession of the suit property; and

(c) Pass any other order as this Hon’ble Court may deem fit and proper.

